Klinefelter syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Tall eunuchoid stature, Small firm testes, Gynaecomastia, 47 XXY karyotype

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-028
Reason for Visit: Acute severe psychosocial distress, panic attack, and suspected medication non-compliance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 19-year-old male with Klinefelter syndrome experiencing a 5-hour, unrelenting panic attack with hyperventilation, chest tightness, and acute suicidal ideation triggered by severe social anxiety and body image distress. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the imminent risk of self-harm, requiring immediate in-person psychiatric de-escalation, acute anxiolysis, and medical evaluation to rule out a cardiac cause for the chest pain.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't breathe, my chest is crushing me, and I just can't look like this or be around people anymore.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 5-hour history of acute, severe social anxiety culminating in a prolonged panic attack. The patient has a known 47,XXY chromosomal pattern. He reports extreme body image distress relating to his tall eunuchoid stature, sparse facial/body hair, and recently worsening gynaecomastia. The current crisis was triggered by an impending social event. The patient admits to abruptly stopping his prescribed testosterone replacement therapy 3 weeks ago due to frustration over perceived lack of rapid physical changes. He now reports generalized weakness, profound social awkwardness, extreme shyness, and a feeling of impending doom. He explicitly endorses passive suicidal ideation ("I don't want to wake up looking like this").
Functional Status: Ambulant; currently incapacitated by severe psychological distress and panic.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute self-harm; profound social withdrawal and treatment non-compliance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85.0 Kg
General: Patient is visibly trembling, hyperventilating, tearful, and avoiding eye contact.
Physical Exam: Physical exam reveals tall stature with disproportionately long arms and legs (eunuchoid proportions). Gynaecomastia is visibly present. Genitourinary exam (consented to rule out acute testicular torsion as a pain source, though chest was primary complaint) confirms small firm testes (microorchidism). Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops; chest wall is non-tender to palpation. Respiratory exam is clear, but breathing is rapid and shallow (hyperventilation). Neurological exam shows poor muscle tone.
Environmental Observations: The patient's room is dark; testosterone gel packets are found unopened in the trash bin.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been non-compliant with prescribed topical testosterone replacement therapy for 3 weeks.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Klinefelter syndrome (complicated by acute panic attack and testosterone withdrawal) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome/Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the young age, absence of hypoxia or risk factors for DVT, and the clear, overwhelming psychological trigger perfectly correlating with the onset of the chest tightness (panic).

Primary Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole primary diagnosis, because the mood symptoms and panic are directly, mechanistically tied to the endocrine withdrawal and syndromic body-image distress of his genetic condition.

Acute Psychosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of auditory/visual hallucinations or delusions; his distress is based on an accurate, albeit catastrophized, perception of his physical reality.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Suicidal ideation and treatment non-compliance)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a severe panic attack with chest tightness and suicidal ideation required urgent medical and psychiatric evaluation. The objective findings of sinus tachycardia, hyperventilation, and classic syndromic features (gynaecomastia, eunuchoid proportions) in the context of abrupt testosterone cessation confirmed an acute psychological crisis exacerbated by acute hormone withdrawal. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the immediate risk of self-harm required urgent in-person safety planning, acute pharmacological anxiolysis to break the physical panic cycle, and immediate re-initiation of essential endocrine therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg to rapidly abort the acute panic attack and reduce the hyperventilation. Conducted a thorough suicide risk assessment and established a verbal safety contract with the patient and his mother. Supervised the immediate re-application of his prescribed topical testosterone gel.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you apply the testosterone gel immediately after brushing your teeth every morning, you link the medication to an established habit; therefore, building this strict routine guarantees the steady hormone levels required to improve your muscle tone and mood. When you practice the "box breathing" technique for 2 minutes as soon as your chest feels tight, you manually slow your heart rate; utilizing this tool rewards you with immediate physical control over the panic.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community mental health triage for psychological support regarding body dysmorphia.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP Telehealth review tomorrow morning to assess mood stability and ensure ongoing medication compliance.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the urge to self-harm becomes active and uncontrollable, if the chest pain becomes crushing and radiates to the jaw or arm, or if the panic attack returns and cannot be stopped with breathing exercises.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Hormone replacement therapy requires months to show maximal physical effects; abrupt cessation causes rapid, severe mood destabilization.
Clinical Course: The diazepam will provide relief from the physical panic within an hour. Mood stability will gradually return over the next 1-2 weeks as steady-state testosterone levels are re-established.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The mother must secure all potentially harmful objects in the home and monitor the patient closely overnight. Seek urgent care if the patient expresses active plans to end his life.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Stopping the testosterone abruptly caused a massive hormone crash in your brain, which severely worsened your anxiety and triggered the panic attack. The diazepam acts as a temporary circuit breaker for the panic, but restarting the daily testosterone is the actual treatment for your mood and muscle weakness.
Actionable Steps: Never stop the hormone therapy abruptly. Apply the gel daily. Practice box breathing (inhale 4s, hold 4s, exhale 4s, hold 4s). Engage with the mental health referral.

